## Details

In this demo, we try to transform each pixel from an image into a set of $K$ representative colors. The $K$ colors are selected to reconstruct the original image with minimal error. It is basically a compression algorithm where, instead of each pixel representing a 32-bit number, it is _quantized_ into just $K$ distinct colors. For example, if $K = 8$, then each color can be encoded with 3 bits ($\log_2 8 = 3$) instead of 32.

We use two algorithms for this demo: [K-means][kmeans] and a simplified version of a [Gaussian mixture model][gaussian].

Here we use an isotropic Gaussian, which means that the covariance matrix is a scalar multiple of the identity matrix.

The probability density function of the simplified mixture model is:

$$f(x) = \sum_{i=1}^{K} \frac{\phi_i}{(2\pi)^{d/2} \sigma_i^d} \exp\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma_i^2}\|x - \mu_i\|^2\right)$$

Here, $\phi_i$ ($\phi_i > 0$ and $\sum_{i=1}^{K} \phi_i = 1$) is the weight of the $i$-th Gaussian component, $\mu_i$ is the mean vector of the $i$-th Gaussian component, and $\sigma_i^2$ is the variance of the $i$-th Gaussian component.



[kmeans]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-means_clustering
[gaussian]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-means_clustering#Gaussian_mixture_model
